WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370 align:start position:0%
 
I'm joined today by dr. Elizabeth  Englander who's a professor of  psychology and the director of the  Massachusetts aggression reduction  Center at Bridgewater State University  which is one of the leading research  centers in the u.s. that looks at  cyberbullying so first question I think  is when we look at cyberbullying in what  ways do we need to look at it  differently than in-person bullying and  in what ways is it the same if there are  any well there are some differences I  think the difference is primarily are  usually around how people use digital  technology when they communicate so for  example saying something online is  really not the same as saying it in  person there's often a lot of  information missing and we noticed that  in our subjects there are a lot of  misunderstandings that lead to problems  between kids online as well as things  that sort of get blown up out of  proportion but having said that I think  it's really important to not think about  cyber bullying and bullying as being  completely separate I think they usually  interact with each other in kids lives  today and online is really sort of just  another place where kids see their  friends and so there's really a lot  going on online that also happens in  school and it tends to go back and forth  I don't know if you've followed at all  this situation called gamergate that's  been going on which focuses a lot around  online a harassment that different  groups allege they've been the victims  of or that different people have been  perpetrators of and one of the  interesting narratives that has  surrounded it has been that some say if  gamergate rather than being an online  thing was an in-person thing you  wouldn't have almost any of the  harassment because by their very nature  many of the video gamers that are  involved in this issue are not the types  of people who would have the  personalities to participate in  in-person bullying I'm curious whether  you have any thoughts on that and again  I don't know how much you know  specifically about this gamergate thing  but generally how does that sound to you  that sounds very plausible to me one of  the things that changes when people go  online there's a few things  change but one of the important things  that changes is that people tend to  become very disinhibited so they'll say  and do things that they usually would  not do in person and there can be a  couple of different reasons for that  sometimes when you're online you  interact with other people who agree  with you primarily and that can sort of  embolden you  whereas in person you can't always  control so much who's listening to you  and who's talking with you and so people  tend to be a little more tentative but  also online it's there's a very strong  tendency when you're interacting with a  machine that has a person attached to  the other end to sort of forget that  there are people at the other end and to  over focus on yourself so a user might  say and do things in an effort to maybe  impress their friends or to look as  though they're really intelligent or  really informed and they sort of forget  that there's another person at the other  end and we see that all the time I want  to talk a little bit about the the  victims of physical and cyber bullying  but before we get to that when we think  about the reasons why someone may bully  or harass others do we see any  difference in sort of the catalyst or  impetus for bullying and harassment  online versus in person well it it does  strike me that a lot of the online  incidents that we study a higher  proportion of them seem to be a little  more inadvertent it's not that people do  them by accident  kids are still may be mean to somebody  they may say something mean on purpose  but they often don't intend for it to be  repeated quite so much they may not  intend for it to be emphasized they may  not intend for it to have quite the  impact it has and because they can't see  the other person they don't know what  kind of impact it's having and so they  can't sort of adjust their narrative so  they're really in person you see a lot  more things that are more deliberate  maybe more thought-out  but online there seems to be a lot more  communication problems that happen  because something gets out of hand or a  child starts a rumor and suddenly  everyone's talking about it maybe they  didn't intend that to happen so we  really see a difference in intent online  a lot of the time the impact of bullying  on the victim can really vary  drastically even for for in-person  bullying or online bullying are there  any more general observations that you  have about how victims of in-person  bullying may be differently affected  than victims of online bullying well  that's kind of controversial at the  moment okay there's not a lot of good  data that really cuts it either way very  clearly there are some studies that have  found that in-person bullying is more  impactful and there are other studies  that have found that what happens online  is really much more impactful we do know  that some people are much more resilient  to these kinds of problems there are  some there are some kids who really seem  to let this stuff slide off their back  but many others are very impacted and  it's it's important to note that these  kinds of social attacks are really  associated with problems like depression  and anxiety and and social problems and  academic and work problems and and they  have a very significant cost but it can  be really hard to predict what's going  to impact a particular person the most  so if you have one person that person  might say oh well you know I was much  more impacted but what happened in  school but another child might say oh no  it wasn't in school that was big the big  thing was having you know an  embarrassing photo posted that everyone  in the world could see so we really  don't know very much about the  difference and why it is that sometimes  one thing seems to hit kids harder and  sometimes the other thing I mean I can  certainly imagine how if you are at home  on a computer you might have more of an  expectation of immunity to bullying than  if you were in a public place with other  people physically around you right  I could understand feeling like hey if  I'm at home my parents are home I'm just  on my computer you might be less  prepared or expecting to be the victim  of bullying yeah you might feel safer  you might feel like mmm you know I don't  know if if children today really  imagined that bullying only happens in  school anymore I think that children  today are pretty aware that it happens  online so they don't I don't think they  have the expectation that it's only  gonna happen in school the way kids  maybe 5 or 10 years ago might have kids  today are very very aware of cyber  bullying on the whole but having said  that it does feel different it may feel  much more invasive when you're at home  and cyber bullying does have the unique  characteristic that it doesn't depend on  where you are so it doesn't stop at the  end of school it can go on all the time  and that the relentless nature of that  is really hard probably the thing that  we see that hurts kids the most is when  these kinds of it social problems are  repeated when somebody goes after them  again and again it's not the one time  things that seem to stick with kids it's  the times when a social problem happens  over and over again that are really  important last thing I want to talk  about is sort of a pragmatic approach to  getting would be bullies or former  bullies to stop bullying you know we've  talked to a lot of different experts in  child development who who point out the  sort of longer term problems that can be  caused by a more authoritarian parenting  style and so I'm curious as to whether  you what what observations you might  have about effective techniques for  getting bullies not to bully our  punishments our harsh punishments really  the way to go or does it correlate more  with what we've seen about the problems  with a sort of authoritarian response  right well there's also no definitive  answer on that I mean we really don't  understand exactly why people are  aggressive I mean we have ideas in  general we know for example that you  know being pushed around or bullied you  self might lead you to bully others or  having been a victim yourself might be  really important but we can't look at an  individual person and say this is why he  did that  sure what we do know excuse me what we  do know is that when parents take these  things very seriously when they talk to  kids about the idea that there are  really no excuses for being cruel to  other people it doesn't matter if you're  mad it doesn't matter if you're  frustrated there's you never have to  write to do this it's never okay  but those kinds of messages seem to be  most effective and of course they're  really most effective when parents model  that themselves so when parents  themselves aren't cruel to their kids  are cruel to other people and they  really show their kids how to behave to  others we've been speaking with dr.  Elizabeth Englander professor of  psychology and director of the  Massachusetts aggression reduction  Center at Bridgewater State University  super important topic especially as more  and more of life is lived out online or  digitally this is certainly not going to  be an issue that goes away so thanks so  much for talking to us about it thanks  for having me

